# Kubernetes Roles and ServiceAccount Demo

This folder demonstrates how Kubernetes Role-Based Access Control (RBAC) can be used to limit what a pod is allowed to do.

## What this setup does

- Creates a ServiceAccount named `limited-access-sa` in the `access` namespace.
- Binds that ServiceAccount to a Role using a RoleBinding.
- Runs a pod that uses that ServiceAccount so the workload inherits only the permissions granted by the Role.

This is a least-privilege pattern: instead of giving a pod broad cluster permissions, only specific actions are allowed.

## Files in this folder

- `serviceaccount.yaml` – creates the ServiceAccount.
- `rolebinding.yaml` – connects the ServiceAccount to a Role.
- `pod-running-limited-sa.yaml` – runs a pod using the restricted ServiceAccount.

## Apply the manifests

```bash
kubectl apply -f serviceaccount.yaml
kubectl apply -f rolebinding.yaml
kubectl apply -f pod-running-limited-sa.yaml
```

## Purpose

This pattern is useful when you want a workload to access only limited Kubernetes resources, such as reading or updating specific objects in a namespace, without giving it admin-level access.
